          "text": "using this TRD document i wrote as input, rewrite the README.md and CLAUDE.md files to document the project\n\n# ‘use-ai’: Frontend AI\n\n👥 Teams: 基盤 / Foundation (https://www.notion.so/Foundation-8e088457830842569dad161b2b2d4031?pvs=21)\nAuthors: Zachary Davison\nDate: November 6, 2025 1:00 PM\nTRD Status: In Review\nLast edited by: Zachary Davison\nLast edited time: November 6, 2025 2:44 PM\n\n---\n\n# 🤖 How AI was used in this document\n\n<aside>\n🤖\n\nAI was used to generate the Sequence Diagram.\n\nAll other text was written by a human.\n\n</aside>\n\n# :github: Repository\n\n<aside>\n<img src=\"notion://custom_emoji/95b87a08-3df0-4e56-ab6b-94065867041b/2781ff99-6922-80ef-890d-007a0edf762e\" alt=\"notion://custom_emoji/95b87a08-3df0-4e56-ab6b-94065867041b/2781ff99-6922-80ef-890d-007a0edf762e\" width=\"40px\" />\n\n[https://github.com/meetsmore/use-ai](https://github.com/meetsmore/use-ai)\n\n</aside>\n\n# 👁️ Overview\n\nWe want to provide an AI chat widget on select pages of ProOne and MeetsMore so that users can perform actions on the page using AI.\n\nThis is similar to [Notion AI](https://www.notion.com/help/guides/category/ai), where the user can ask it to take actions on the page, or to summarize the page.\n\nIn order to easily provide this functionality, we want the actions to be taken to invoke functions on the frontend.\n\nIt may be necessary to provide backend capabilities in future as well, although this may not be necessary for V1.\n\n![image.png](%E2%80%98use-ai%E2%80%99%20Frontend%20AI/image.png)\n\n# 📝 [WIP] Requirements\n\n<aside>\n⚠️\n\n[zac] This section is WIP.\n\n</aside>\n\n| MUST | Requirement |\n| --- | --- |\n| ✅ | Users can control supported parts of the frontend UI of meetsmore products with a chat interface. |\n| ✅ | Developers can add AI functionality to a page easily. |\n| ✅ | Developers can add AI functionality to individual UI components (so they can be re-used). |\n| ✅ | Users must not be able to abuse the chat interface to waste MeetsMore LLM credits. |\n|  | Invisible (Provider) components should be supported. |\n\n# 🧪 Existing Tools\n\n## WebMCP\n\n<aside>\n❓\n\nWebMCP is an [open source](https://github.com/jasonjmcghee/WebMCP) JavaScript library that allows any website to integrate with the Model Context Protocol. It provides a small blue widget in the bottom right corner of your page that allows users to connect to and interact with your webpage via LLM or agent.\n\n</aside>\n\nWebMCP is a [proposed standard](https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp) and small library for providing the capability for LLMs to control web interfaces.\n\nThe de-facto implementation of this standard is currently [mcp-b](https://mcp-b.ai/).\n\n## MCP-B\n\n[mcp-b](https://mcp-b.ai/) is an implementation of the WebMCP proposed standard. \n\nIt provides the capability for the frontend to expose an MCP server so that any LLM can control it.\n\nThe core functionality is there, but in order to get the development experience we want, we will have to build on-top of it.\n\n# 🧑🏼‍💼 Implementation\n\n## Architecture\n\n![image.png](%E2%80%98use-ai%E2%80%99%20Frontend%20AI/image%201.png)\n\nThe functionality is generic, so will be provided by a set of libraries called `use-ai` (`@use-ai/client` & `@use-ai/server`).\n\nThe main components are:\n\n- `UseAIProvider` → A React component which handles the setup for connecting to a `use-ai` server.\n- `UseAIServer` → A server class which brokers the connection to the LLM via websockets, and handles state transitions for tool invocations.\n- `useAI` → A React hook which takes a list of tools and a declarative state, and provides those tools as MCP tools to the LLM via `UseAIProvider` and `UseAIServer`.\n\n## Sequence Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n      participant Client as Client (UseAIProvider)\n      participant Server as Server (UseAIServer)\n      participant LLM as Claude API\n      Note over Client,Server: 1. Connection & Tool Registration\n      Client->>Server: WebSocket connect\n      activate Server\n      Server-->>Client: Connection established\n      Client->>Server: register_tools\n      Note over Client,Server: Send tool definitions as JSON Schema\n      Server-->>Client: ACK\n      Note over Client,LLM: 2. Send Prompt (and component state)\n      Client->>Server: prompt message\n      Note over Server: Include conversation history (cached)\n      Server->>LLM: messages.create with tools\n      activate LLM\n      Note over Server,LLM: 3. LLM Decides to Use Tools\n      LLM-->>Server: tool_use block\n      Note over Server: Create promise for tool call<br/>Store resolver in pendingToolCalls\n      Server->>Client: tool_call(s) request\n      Note over Client: toolCallId, name, input\n      deactivate LLM\n      Note over Client: Execute tool locally\n      Note over Client,LLM: 4. Send Tool Response Back\n      Client->>Server: tool_response(s)\n      Note over Server: Resolve promise with result (updated component state)\n      Server->>LLM: messages.create with tool_result\n      activate LLM\n      LLM-->>Server: text response\n      deactivate LLM\n      Server->>Client: response message\n      deactivate Server\n      Note over Client: Update Chat UI with AI response\n```\n\n## Frontend\n\n`@use-ai/client` contains the frontend client for `use-ai`, and contains a very simple interface:\n\n```tsx\nexport default function TodoList() {\n  const [todos, setTodos] = useState<Todo[]>([]);\n  \n  useAI({\n      tools: { addTodo }, // exposes tools to the AI to add todo list items.\n      prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}` // the current state the AI sees.\n  })\n}\n```\n\nTools are defined like so:\n\n```tsx\nconst addTodo = defineTool(\n            // description, so the AI knows what it is\n      'Add a new todo item to the list',\n      // zod schema for validation\n      z.object({\n        text: z.string().describe('The text content of the todo item'),\n      }),\n      // actual function invoked when AI calls this tool\n      (input) => {\n          // reject empty todos\n        if (!input.text.trim()) {\n          return { success: false, error: 'Text is required' };\n        }\n           \n          // create the todo list item from the input\n        const newTodo: Todo = {\n          id: nextIdRef.current++,\n          text: input.text.trim(),\n          completed: false,\n        };\n\n                // update component state\n        setTodos(prev => [...prev, newTodo]);\n        \n        // inform the LLM it was successful, and include the final result.\n        return { success: true, id: newTodo.id, message: `Added todo: \"${input.text}\"` };\n      }\n    );\n```\n\nPowering this is `UseAIProvider`, which manages the connection and global config:\n\n```tsx\n  root.render(\n      <UseAIProvider\n        serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n        systemPrompt=\"Be concise and friendly in your responses.\"\n      >\n            <App />\n      </UseAIProvider>\n  );\n```\n\n`UseAIProvider` also manages the ‘AI Chat Button’ and the ‘AI Chat Pane’.\n\nAll child components within the scope of a `UseAIProvider` instance will have their `useAI` hook tools aggregated, so there is only one chat pane, but it knows about all tools that are currently rendered on the page.\n\n### Handling multiple components of same type\n\nYou can define an `id` for a `useAI` hook, and it will be used to prefix all tools made available to the MCP.\n\n```tsx\n  function ListItem({ rowIndex, initialLabel, initialColor }: ListItemProps) {\n      useAI({\n        tools,\n        prompt: `Current state - Label: \"${label}\", Counter: ${counter}, Color: \"${color}\".`,\n        id: `Row ${rowIndex}`\n      });\n    })\n```\n\nThis is usually enough for the AI to differentiate between which components it is targeting.\n\nIf your component has an `id` attribute, it will be automatically used as the `id`.\n\n```tsx\n<ListItem id='Row 1'/>\n```\n\n### Component State\n\nComponent state is provided to the AI via the `prompt` argument of a `useAI` hook.\n\nSince this argument is a dependency of the hook, it will cause a re-render of the hook whenever the component re-renders.\n\nThe AI only receives state of the components that have `useAI` hooks, and when a tool call is invoked, it only receives the state of the component affected in the tool response.\n\nThis allows the user to use the application normally, changing component state, and jump in & out of using the AI chat as they need it.\n\n`use-ai` waits for a component to re-render before receiving the response for a tool call, so it has the latest state available to it.\n\n<aside>\n💡\n\nOriginally, the LLM received the full HTML rendering of the component.\n\nThis was wasteful however, and resulted in unnecessary tokens being sent to the LLM (increasing context window size and token cost), so a plain string, chosen by the developer, is used instead.\n\n</aside>\n\n### Invisible Components\n\nSince `use-ai` waits for a component to re-render before responding to an LLMs tool call(s), this means that components that **don’t** re-render as a result of a tool call will cause the LLM to never receive a response.\n\nThis breaks a use case whereby we define a `Provider` that provides tools to an AI, but doesn’t actually render anything.\n\nThese components are supported via the `invisible` flag:\n\n```tsx\n  function EmailProvider({ apiKey }: EmailProviderProps) {\n      useAI({\n        tools: { sendEmail }\n        invisible: true // means the use-ai will not wait for a re-render\n      });\n    })\n```\n\n### Multi-Tool Use\n\nA feature which enables a very useful emergent behavior is the support for ‘multi-tool use’, where the Anthropic LLM will return multiple tool calls in one response.\n\n`use-ai` supports this, and as a result, you can get ‘bulk’ action functionality simply by supporting individual mutations:\n\n```tsx\nexport default function TodoList() {\n  const [todos, setTodos] = useState<Todo[]>([]);\n  \n  useAI({\n      tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n      prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n  })\n}\n```\n\nWith just these two tools (`addTodo` and `deleteTodo`), we can ask the AI to do things like:\n\n<aside>\n🤖\n\n‘add a shopping list to make tonkotsu ramen’\n\n</aside>\n\n…and the LLM will invoke `addTodo` multiple times to add all the items in the shopping list.\n\n### Customizing the UI\n\nYou can customize the AI UI by passing custom components to `UseAIProvider`:\n\n```tsx\n  <UseAIProvider\n    serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n    CustomButton=MyCustomButton // custom floating button\n    CustomChat=MyCustomChat // custom chat UI\n  >\n```\n\n### Zod Validation & Type Safety\n\nTool definitions are defined with [Zod](https://zod.dev/) schemas:\n\n```tsx\nconst addTodo = defineTool(\n      'Add a new todo item to the list',\n      // zod schema for validation\n      z.object({\n        text: z.string().describe('The text content of the todo item'),\n      }),\n      (input: {text: string} ) => { /* logic */ }\n    );\n```\n\nThese are converted to [JSONSchema](https://json-schema.org/) to be provided as definition for the MCP tool definition passed to the LLM, but also used for type safety (must match the function arguments) and validation (validating the LLMs tool call).\n\nIf a tool does not take arguments, you don’t need to provide a zod schema:\n\n```tsx\nconst logout = defineTool(\n    'Log the current user out.',\n    () => { /* logout logic */ }\n)\n```\n\n### Dangerous Actions\n\nYou can attempt to mitigate against dangerous tool calls using the `confirmationRequired` argument in a tool:\n\n```tsx\nconst deleteMyAccount = defineTool(\n    'Delete this users account permanently.',\n    () => { /* dangerous deletion logic */ },\n    { confirmationRequired: true }  // ask the LLM to confirm before taking action\n)\n```\n\nThis is not 100% foolproof, as it simply aggressively prompts the AI to always ask for confirmation before taking an action, the AI may ignore this.\n\n```tsx\n    const confirmationTools = session.tools.filter(tool => tool.confirmationRequired);\n    if (confirmationTools.length > 0) {\n      if (parts.length > 0) {\n        parts.push('');\n      }\n      parts.push('CRITICAL: The following tools require user confirmation EVERY TIME before execution:');\n      parts.push('');\n      confirmationTools.forEach(tool => {\n        parts.push(`- ${tool.name}`);\n      });\n      parts.push('');\n      parts.push('MANDATORY CONFIRMATION WORKFLOW:');\n      parts.push('1. First, explain to the user exactly what changes will be made (which specific items will be affected)');\n      parts.push('2. Then, explicitly ask: \"Do you want me to proceed?\" or similar confirmation question');\n      parts.push('3. STOP and WAIT for the user to respond with explicit confirmation (like \"yes\", \"ok\", \"proceed\")');\n      parts.push('4. ONLY after receiving confirmation in the IMMEDIATE user message, call the tool');\n      parts.push('');\n      parts.push('NEVER ASSUME CONFIRMATION:');\n      parts.push('- Do NOT call these tools without receiving explicit confirmation in the user\\'s most recent message');\n      parts.push('- Previous confirmations in the conversation history do NOT count as confirmation for new operations');\n      parts.push('- Each deletion or destructive operation requires its own separate confirmation');\n      parts.push('- If the user\\'s message does not contain explicit confirmation, ask for it again');\n    }\n```\n\n### ‘Extra’ functionality\n\nBehind the scenes, there are additional features:\n\n- Prompt caching: [Anthropic prompt caching](https://claude.com/blog/prompt-caching) is supported to save token cost.\n\n## Backend\n\nThe backend is a small node.js server running on `bun`, that hosts a `UseAIServer` class instance.\n\nThis will be deployed separately to the main MeetsMore and ProOne applications, and can be horizontally scaled.\n\n## Security\n\n### Rate Limiting\n\n`UseAIServer` supports rate limiting:\n\n```tsx\nconst rateLimitMaxRequests = Number(process.env.RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS) || 0;\nconst rateLimitWindowMs = Number(process.env.RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS) || 60000;\n```\n\nYou can specify a per-websocket connection rate limit, and a rate limit window.\n\nEach websocket connection will only allow that many requests within that rate limit window.\n\nThis is useful so that we can prevent abuse of our AI (e.g. using our chat window as a way to access LLM for free).\n\n### Authorization\n\n<aside>\n⚠️\n\nAuthorization is not implemented yet.\n\n</aside>\n\nIt may be desirable for users to need to be logged in to use `use-ai` chat, either to use it at all, or to allow them to have a higher rate limit when logged in.\n\nThis can be supported through JWTs that will be vended from our main applications (P1 & MM).\n\n### ‘[The Lethal Trifecta](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/)’\n\n<aside>\n🗨️\n\nIf you are a user of LLM systems that use tools (you can call them “AI agents” if you like) it is *critically* important that you understand the risk of combining tools with the following three characteristics. Failing to understand this **can let an attacker steal your data**.\n\nThe **lethal trifecta** of capabilities is:\n\n- **Access to your private data**—one of the most common purposes of tools in the first place!\n- **Exposure to untrusted content**—any mechanism by which text (or images) controlled by a malicious attacker could become available to your LLM\n- **The ability to externally communicate** in a way that could be used to steal your data (I often call this “exfiltration” but I’m not confident that term is widely understood.)\n</aside>\n\nWe propose the following mitigations:\n\n1. [**Exposure to untrusted content / The ability to externally communicate]** `use-ai` server cannot invoke tools to access the wider internet (no curl, search, etc).\n2. [**Access to your private data]** `use-ai` V1 cannot access backend APIs.\n\nEssentially, `use-ai` can only see what’s on the page, and can’t look at anywhere else on the internet.\n\nIt can also only take actions that the user can already do (because they are in frontend javascript already).\n\n### No Backend API Access\n\nWe propose initially not to provide any capability for `use-ai` to integrate with backend APIs.\n\nIt was initially suggested that we provide a NestJS decorator `Mcp()` that would provide that endpoint as a tool for our MM/P1 APIs, but we suggest not doing that for V1.\n\n1. It’s possible to provide a large amount of capability with only the frontend `use-ai` functionality.\n2. There is a risk that `Mcp()` decorator could be used too freely and expose AI to an endpoint that it could abuse.\n3. It increases the complexity of the deployment, because the MCP definition for the backend must also be provided to `use-ai` server.\n\n## Deployment / Infrastructure\n\n`@use-ai/server` will be deployed on Kubernetes as a service.\n\nThe server has in-memory state only (active websocket sessions), so can be horizontally scaled easily and shared across both products.\n\nThe only difference in deployment for P1 & MM may be that the JWT signing keys would be different, but this can be resolved and both can be supported if necessary.\n\n## [TBD] Scale\n\n<aside>\n⚠️\n\n[zac] I would like to know if we have any information about expected users on P1 and MM.\n\nHow many active users of frontend P1 do we have? How many do we estimate will use AI?\n\n</aside>\n\n## Testing\n\n`use-ai` itself has tests, but integration tests should be written for the products that use it.\n\nE2E UI tests with real Anthropic keys should be used, unless the cost becomes too expensive, because the AI can be unpredictable and we want to catch those edge-cases.\n\nIf necessary, we can provide a mock Anthropic interface for `use-ai` so QA team can control the responses exactly.\n\nEach page with AI features should have integration (E2E UI) tests authored. QA team will need to be involved.\n\n## [TBD] Candidate Product Features\n\n<aside>\n⚠️\n\n[zac] I need feedback on where would be the best first place to try out `use-ai` would be in ProOne.\n\n</aside>\n\n## Open Source\n\n`use-ai` is a candidate for open source, since it is generic.\n\nI would be happy to support it as an open source project, but I need permission from MeetsMore first.\n\nGiven that @柄澤史也 / Fumiya Karasawa indicated we might try and sell this product, this may not be possible.\n\n# ❓Open Questions\n\n# 💬 Feedback",
