            "content": "# CLAUDE.md\n\nThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n\n## Project Overview\n\n`use-ai` is a TypeScript monorepo for building AI-powered React applications with Notion AI-like functionality. The library enables React components to expose tools (functions) to Claude AI via a WebSocket server, allowing users to control UI components through natural language prompts.\n\n**Key Innovation**: Tools are defined and executed on the client where application state lives, with the server acting as a coordinator between Claude API and client tools. This enables direct access to React state, browser APIs, and local data without backend dependencies.\n\n## Development Commands\n\n### Setup\n```bash\nbun install                    # Install dependencies\n```\n\n### Development\n```bash\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...  # Required for server\nbun run start:server           # Start AI server (port 8080)\nbun run dev                    # Start example app with hot reload (port 3000)\n```\n\nThe server must be running before starting the example app.\n\n### Logging Configuration\n```bash\nexport LOG_FORMAT=pretty       # Development: human-readable logs (default)\nexport LOG_FORMAT=json         # Production: structured JSON logs\n```\n\n### Rate Limiting Configuration\n```bash\nexport RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=10      # Max requests per window (0 = unlimited)\nexport RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000      # Rate limit window in ms (default: 1 minute)\n```\n\n### Building\n```bash\nbun run build                  # Build all packages (client, server, example)\nbun run build:client           # Build @use-ai/client only\nbun run build:server           # Build @use-ai/server only\n```\n\nBuild process: Bun bundles code → TypeScript generates type declarations → ESM output\n\n### Testing\n```bash\nbun test                       # Run all unit tests\nbun run test:e2e               # Run E2E tests (requires ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)\nbun run test:e2e:ui            # Interactive E2E test UI\nbun run test:e2e:headed        # E2E tests with visible browser\nbun run test:e2e:debug         # E2E tests in debug mode\n```\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Monorepo Structure\n- `packages/client/` - `@use-ai/client`: React hooks library for AI integrations\n- `packages/server/` - `@use-ai/server`: WebSocket server for AI agent hosting\n- `apps/example/` - Example AI-powered todo list demonstrating the library\n\n### Core Data Flow\n\n```\n1. Tool Registration\n   Client registers tools → Server stores tool definitions\n\n2. User Prompt + Component State\n   User sends prompt → Client includes component state via `prompt` argument\n   → Server forwards to Claude API with tool definitions\n\n3. AI Processing & Tool Selection\n   Claude analyzes prompt + state → Returns tool_use blocks\n\n4. Tool Execution (Client-Side)\n   Server sends tool_call to client → Client executes tool locally\n   → Client waits for component re-render (to get updated state)\n   → Client sends tool_response with new state back to server\n\n5. Final Response\n   Server sends tool results to Claude → Claude generates response\n   → Server streams response to client\n```\n\n### Key Architectural Patterns\n\n#### Client-Side Tool Execution\n- Tools are **defined and executed on the client** (where application state lives)\n- Server acts as **coordinator** between Claude API and client tools\n- This enables tools to access React state, browser APIs, and local data directly\n- **Security benefit**: No backend API access means AI can only see/manipulate what's on the page\n\n#### Component State Management\n- Component state is provided to AI via the `prompt` argument of `useAI` hook\n- The AI only receives state from components using `useAI`, keeping context focused\n- Library waits for component re-render before sending tool response to ensure latest state\n- State is serialized to string (developer-controlled, not full HTML rendering)\n\nExample:\n```typescript\nuseAI({\n  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`  // Current state AI sees\n});\n```\n\n#### Invisible Components\nComponents that provide tools but don't re-render (e.g., providers) use the `invisible` flag:\n\n```typescript\nuseAI({\n  tools: { sendEmail },\n  invisible: true  // Don't wait for re-render after tool calls\n});\n```\n\nWithout this flag, the library would wait indefinitely for a re-render that never comes.\n\n#### Multiple Components of Same Type\nUse `id` parameter to differentiate component instances:\n\n```typescript\nfunction ListItem({ rowIndex }) {\n  useAI({\n    tools: { updateLabel, incrementCounter },\n    id: `Row ${rowIndex}`  // Prefixes all tool names\n  });\n}\n```\n\nTools are registered as `Row 1/updateLabel`, `Row 2/updateLabel`, etc.\n\nIf component has `id` attribute, it's automatically used:\n```tsx\n<ListItem id=\"Row 1\" />\n```\n\n#### WebSocket Protocol\nAll messages follow `{ type: string, data: object }` format:\n\n**Client → Server:**\n- `register_tools`: Send tool definitions (JSON Schema format)\n- `prompt`: Send user prompt with optional conversation ID\n- `tool_response`: Return tool execution result for a specific tool call\n\n**Server → Client:**\n- `tool_call`: Request tool execution with `{ toolCallId, name, input }`\n- `response`: Final AI response with conversation ID\n- `error`: Error messages\n\n#### Promise-Based Tool Coordination\nThe server uses promises to synchronously wait for async tool execution across the WebSocket boundary:\n\n```typescript\n// Server creates promise and stores resolver\nconst promise = new Promise(resolve => {\n  session.pendingToolCalls.set(toolCallId, resolve);\n});\n\n// Send tool_call to client\nthis.sendMessage(ws, 'tool_call', { toolCallId, name, input });\n\n// Wait for client to execute and send tool_response\nconst result = await promise;\n```\n\nThis enables synchronous-style code despite async WebSocket communication.\n\n#### Conversation History Management\n- Server maintains **full conversation history per WebSocket session**\n- History includes user messages, assistant messages (with tool_use blocks), and tool results\n- Enables multi-turn conversations with context preservation\n- History is lost on disconnect (session-based, not persisted to database)\n- **Prompt caching**: Anthropic prompt caching automatically used for repeated conversations\n\n#### Tool Aggregation Pattern\n- `UseAIProvider` maintains a registry of tools from all mounted `useAI` hooks\n- Tools from multiple components are merged into a single set\n- Each hook gets a unique ID for registration/cleanup\n- Enables compositional tool registration across component tree\n- Single chat interface knows about all tools currently rendered on page\n\n### Tool Definition System\n\nTools are defined using `defineTool()` with Zod schemas:\n\n```typescript\nimport { defineTool } from '@use-ai/client';\nimport { z } from 'zod';\n\n// With arguments\nconst addTodo = defineTool(\n  'Add a new todo item to the list',\n  z.object({ text: z.string() }),\n  (input) => {\n    // Implementation with typed input\n    return { success: true, message: 'Todo added' };\n  }\n);\n\n// Without arguments\nconst logout = defineTool(\n  'Log the current user out',\n  () => { /* logout logic */ }\n);\n\n// With confirmation required (dangerous actions)\nconst deleteAccount = defineTool(\n  'Delete this user\\'s account permanently',\n  () => { /* deletion logic */ },\n  { confirmationRequired: true }\n);\n```\n\n**Under the hood:**\n- Zod schema is converted to JSON Schema (via `zod-to-json-schema`) for Claude API\n- Original Zod schema is retained for runtime validation\n- `_execute()` method validates input with Zod before calling function\n- TypeScript types are inferred from Zod schema\n- `confirmationRequired` option aggressively prompts AI to ask for confirmation (not 100% foolproof)\n\n### Multi-Tool Use Support\n\nThe library supports Claude's multi-tool use feature, enabling emergent \"bulk action\" behavior:\n\n```typescript\nuseAI({\n  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n});\n```\n\nUser: \"add a shopping list to make tonkotsu ramen\"\n\nClaude will automatically call `addTodo` multiple times for each ingredient, even though only single-item operations are defined. This is a powerful emergent behavior.\n\n### State Synchronization Pattern\n\nThe example app demonstrates a pub/sub pattern for keeping UI in sync when tools mutate state:\n\n```typescript\nconst listeners = new Set<() => void>();\n\nexport function subscribeTodos(listener: () => void) {\n  listeners.add(listener);\n  return () => listeners.delete(listener);\n}\n\nfunction notifyListeners() {\n  listeners.forEach(fn => fn());\n}\n\n// Tools call notifyListeners() after state mutations\n```\n\nThis enables both AI operations and manual UI operations to update the same state.\n\n### Custom UI Support\n\nDefault chat UI can be replaced with custom components:\n\n```typescript\n<UseAIProvider\n  serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n  CustomButton={MyCustomButton}  // Replace floating button\n  CustomChat={MyCustomChat}      // Replace chat interface\n>\n  <App />\n</UseAIProvider>\n```\n\n## Security\n\n### Rate Limiting\n- Configured via `RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS` and `RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS` env vars\n- Per-connection rate limits prevent abuse of LLM credits\n- Each WebSocket connection is rate-limited independently\n\n### \"Lethal Trifecta\" Mitigations\nFollowing [Simon Willison's security guidance](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/):\n\n1. **Limited internet access**: Server cannot invoke tools to access external internet (no curl, search, etc.)\n2. **No backend API access**: V1 cannot access backend APIs directly\n3. **Client-only scope**: AI can only see what's on the page and take actions users can already perform\n\nThis prevents data exfiltration and limits attack surface.\n\n### Dangerous Actions\nTools can use `confirmationRequired: true` option to aggressively prompt AI for user confirmation before execution. This is not 100% foolproof as AI may ignore prompts.\n\n### No Backend API Access (V1)\nCurrent version intentionally does not provide backend API integration to minimize security risks and complexity. All tools must be implemented in frontend code.\n\n## TypeScript Configuration\n\n- **Strict mode enabled**: All type checks enforced\n- **Module system**: ESM throughout (`\"type\": \"module\"` in all package.json files)\n- **Module resolution**: `\"bundler\"` (Bun-specific)\n- **Target**: ES2022\n- **Never use `any` type unless unavoidable**\n\n## Dependencies\n\n### Client Package\n- `zod` (v4.x): Schema validation and JSON Schema generation\n- `zod-to-json-schema`: Converts Zod schemas to JSON Schema for Claude API\n- `react` (peer dependency): ^18.0.0\n\n### Server Package\n- `@anthropic-ai/sdk`: Claude API integration\n- `ws`: WebSocket server implementation\n\n## Important Implementation Details\n\n### Connection Management\n- Client auto-reconnects on disconnect (5 attempts with exponential backoff)\n- Each `useAI` hook creates a dedicated WebSocket client instance\n- Provider monitors connection status for UI feedback\n\n### Error Handling\n- Tool execution errors are caught and sent back to Claude as `tool_result` content\n- Zod validation errors propagate as tool execution errors\n- Server errors sent as `error` messages to client\n- Client handles errors via optional `onError` callback in `useAI` hook\n\n### Build System\n- Bun is used for both runtime and bundling\n- Package builds: `bun build` creates bundle → `tsc --emitDeclarationOnly` generates types\n- Example app build: `bun build --target browser --minify`\n- All packages output ESM modules\n\n### Environment Variables\n\n**Server:**\n- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (required): Claude API key\n- `LOG_FORMAT` (optional): `pretty` (default) or `json`\n- `RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS` (optional): Max requests per window (0 = unlimited, default: 0)\n- `RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS` (optional): Rate limit window in milliseconds (default: 60000)\n\n**Port Configuration:**\n- Server runs on port 8080 (hardcoded in `packages/server/src/start.ts`)\n- Example app runs on port 3000 (configured in `apps/example/package.json`)\n\n### File Naming Conventions\n- React components: PascalCase (e.g., `UseAIFloatingButton.tsx`)\n- Utilities and hooks: camelCase (e.g., `defineTool.ts`, `useAI.ts`)\n- Main entry points: `index.ts` or `index.tsx`\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n### Unit Tests\n- Located alongside source files (e.g., `defineTool.test.ts`)\n- Run with `bun test`\n- Test individual functions and utilities\n\n### E2E Tests\n- Located in `apps/example/test/`\n- Use Playwright with real Claude API calls\n- Test actual AI interactions including edge cases\n- Require `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable\n- Run with `bun run test:e2e`\n\nE2E tests are preferred for AI features because the AI can be unpredictable and we want to catch edge cases with real API interactions.\n\n## Deployment\n\nThe server is designed to be deployed as a separate service on Kubernetes:\n\n- Has in-memory state only (active WebSocket sessions)\n- Can be horizontally scaled\n- Shared across multiple applications (ProOne, MeetsMore, etc.)\n- Requires only `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable\n- Different products can share same server instance (JWT signing keys may differ)\n\n## Common Development Tasks\n\n### Adding a New Tool\n1. Define tool with `defineTool()` in component\n2. Add tool to `useAI` hook's `tools` object\n3. Update component state in `prompt` argument\n4. Write unit test for tool function\n5. Write E2E test for AI interaction\n\n### Adding a New Feature\n1. Plan implementation (consider client vs server changes)\n2. Update TypeScript types as needed\n3. Implement client-side changes first\n4. Update server if needed (rare for V1)\n5. Add tests (unit + E2E)\n6. Build packages: `bun run build:client` and/or `bun run build:server`\n7. Verify in example app\n\n### Debugging WebSocket Issues\n1. Check server logs (use `LOG_FORMAT=pretty` for readability)\n2. Check browser console for client-side errors\n3. Verify WebSocket connection in browser DevTools Network tab\n4. Check conversation history in server logs\n5. Verify tool registration messages\n\n### Debugging AI Behavior\n1. Check the `prompt` being sent (component state)\n2. Verify tool descriptions are clear and accurate\n3. Check if multi-tool use is causing unexpected behavior\n4. Review conversation history in server logs\n5. Test with E2E tests to reproduce consistently\n\n## Architecture Decisions\n\n### Why Client-Side Tool Execution?\n- Application state lives in React components\n- No need for state synchronization between frontend and backend\n- Direct access to browser APIs and local data\n- Simpler architecture with fewer moving parts\n- Better security (no backend API access in V1)\n\n### Why WebSocket Instead of HTTP?\n- Real-time bidirectional communication needed for tool calls\n- Conversation history maintained per session\n- Lower latency for interactive chat experience\n- Easier to implement streaming responses\n\n### Why No Backend API Access in V1?\n- Reduces security risks (limits \"Lethal Trifecta\")\n- Simpler architecture and deployment\n- Forces developers to think about what AI can access\n- Sufficient for many use cases (UI manipulation, client state)\n\n### Why Zod for Tool Definitions?\n- Type safety at compile time\n- Runtime validation of LLM tool calls\n- Automatic JSON Schema generation for Claude API\n- Great developer experience with TypeScript integration\n\n## Code Organization\n\n- `packages/client/src/components/` - React UI components (chat, button)\n- `packages/client/src/hooks/` - React hooks (`useAI`, `useAIContext`)\n- `packages/client/src/utils/` - Utilities (`defineTool`, WebSocket client)\n- `packages/server/src/` - Server implementation (`UseAIServer` class)\n- `apps/example/src/` - Example app demonstrating features\n- `apps/example/test/` - E2E tests with Playwright\n\n## Future Considerations\n\n### Potential V2 Features (Not Yet Implemented)\n- Backend API integration with authentication/authorization\n- JWT-based user authentication for rate limiting\n- Persistent conversation history (database storage)\n- Tool execution on server side (for backend operations)\n- Support for multiple LLM providers (OpenAI, etc.)\n- Streaming tool execution results\n- Tool composition and chaining\n\n### Known Limitations\n- Conversation history is session-based (lost on disconnect)\n- No authentication/authorization built-in (V1)\n- Rate limiting is per-connection, not per-user\n- `confirmationRequired` is not foolproof (AI may ignore)\n- No support for backend tool execution (V1)\n- Single WebSocket server instance (no clustering with shared state)\n",
